/* One-offs for this chapter only, named .v-* so they read as not-the-system. */

/* s1 + s2 · THE HOLD. One object, one unbroken push, 0 -> 11.924s.

   The phone artwork carries an OPAQUE 640x420 ground layer, so it cannot sit
   on a photograph as an overlay — at any size below full-bleed it is a card
   floating on a second counter, and s1-fix.jpg is itself a phone, which would
   make it a drawn phone on a photographed phone (rule 8). So the Lottie IS the
   picture for both lines, exactly as -hi ch1 plays it, and both .bg divs are
   off rather than left underneath where nobody could ever see them.

   `.v-phone` is deliberately the SAME BOX as `.bg` (inset -8%): the drawn layer
   occupies the photograph's rect, so the plateKen numbers the cut already
   carries transfer unchanged and the push cannot drift. */
#s1-bg, #s2-bg { display: none; }
/* Sized in PIXELS, not `inset: -8%`, and the svg forced to fill. lottie-web
   reads the container's box at loadAnimation() time to size the <svg>; inside a
   scene that has no layout yet it falls back to the artwork's native 640x420
   and pins it top-left, which is what the first encode showed — a phone in the
   corner of an otherwise empty navy frame. ch2's four stages work because they
   carry explicit px. These numbers ARE `.bg`'s -8% box on 1920x1080, so the
   plateKen values still transfer unchanged. */
.v-phone { position: absolute; left: -153.6px; top: -86.4px;
           width: 2227.2px; height: 1252.8px; z-index: 0; overflow: hidden;
           filter: grayscale(0.34) brightness(0.92) contrast(1.05); }
.v-phone svg { display: block; width: 100% !important; height: 100% !important; }

/* s7 · the calendar takes B's right half, sized so lottie-web's default
   `meet` cannot crop a month grid and so it clears .arch-b .huge's 900px
   column (which ends at x=1112). It is z 2 — ABOVE the scrim — because a
   ringed date dimmed by the scrim is the one thing this scene cannot afford. */
.v-cal { position: absolute; left: 1140px; top: 210px; width: 740px; height: 460px; }

/* s6 and s9 · each of these scenes' whole argument is a COUNT, and the global
   `.has-photo.art-forward .art { opacity:.52 !important }` cap is what lost
   s9's streams in the last encode — the per-scene inline opacity the spec
   writes is dead against it. Lifted here, per scene, by id. The global cap is
   untouched and no design token moves. */
#s6-art, #s9-art { opacity: .78 !important; }

/* s2 · the three marks. The line promises three methods; without them the
   scene is 6.4s of a lit phone and a sentence, which is where a viewer goes.
   Placed absolutely rather than in the stack — the stack is lifted verbatim
   from the shipped cut and a chapter spec must not rewrite copy. */
.v-marks { position: absolute; left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%); top: 672px;
           display: flex; gap: 26px; z-index: 3; }
.v-marks i { display: block; width: 58px; height: 58px;
             border: 3px solid var(--fund); border-radius: 4px; }
